{
  /* The fourth project reference, alongside tsconfig.app.json (src/),
     tsconfig.node.json (vite.config.ts) and infra/ (CDK). Same reasoning as
     infra/tsconfig.json: being a reference is what makes `npm run check` and
     `npm run verify` cover this directory without a fourth gate.

     That matters more here than anywhere else in the repo. Since P9 retired Row
     Level Security (ADR 0008), the code under src/data/ *is* the security
     boundary — a TypeScript codebase that tsc skips would be the least
     supervised and most load-bearing code in the project at the same time. */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    /* Node 22 on Lambda. DOM is deliberately absent: this code has no browser
       to run in, and a handler that reaches for `window` or `fetch`'s browser
       overloads should not typecheck. */
    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",

    "composite": true,
    "noEmit": true,

    /* The same strictness as the app and infra. noUncheckedIndexedAccess is
       what makes `result.rows[0]` a `Row | undefined` rather than a `Row` —
       which is precisely the difference between handling "no such row, or not
       yours" and returning undefined as if it were data. */
    "strict": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,

    /* The handlers import the shared Zod schemas and src/lib/day.ts by their
       real relative paths, with extensions — the same bridge the Deno Edge
       Function uses (see supabase/functions/_shared/). One Zod definition per
       concept and one implementation of the day boundary are project rules;
       reaching the real modules is how they stay true. */
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,

    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  /* The two shared modules are listed explicitly because a composite project
     must name every file it compiles (TS6307). They are consequently checked
     twice — once here under Node's lib, once by tsconfig.app.json under the
     DOM's — and that is a feature rather than duplication: both are pure,
     dependency-free modules that genuinely have to work in both runtimes, and
     the second check is what proves it. It is the same arrangement the Deno
     Edge Function relies on through supabase/functions/_shared/.

     Keep this list minimal. Anything added here is a file the API bundle will
     pull in, and a module that reaches for the DOM will fail this project
     rather than the app's. */
  "include": [
    "src",
    "../../src/lib/schemas.ts",
    "../../src/lib/day.ts"
  ]
}
